rules_version = '2';

// Verified 13 Aug 2026 via the Firebase Rules API (projects/convolab-490517
// /rulesets and /releases both returned {}): this project has NEVER had a
// Firestore ruleset, in the console or anywhere else. Not "rules exist and
// live only in the console" as prior notes assumed -- there is no ruleset at
// all. This file is the first one.
//
// This is NOT currently a live vulnerability. Every Firestore access in this
// app goes through packages/api using @google-cloud/firestore with a service
// account (Application Default Credentials / IAM), which is Admin-SDK-style
// access and BYPASSES these rules entirely by design -- rules only govern
// client SDKs (firebase/firestore) authenticating as an end user. Confirmed
// the frontend (packages/app, packages/landing) has zero "firebase/firestore"
// or "firebase/app" imports and "firebase" is not a dependency of either.
//
// So today, deploying this file changes nothing observable: no code path
// currently relies on rules-governed access. What it does is close the gap
// for the future -- if anyone ever adds client-side Firebase SDK access (a
// quick prototype, an admin tool, a mobile app), they hit a database with an
// explicit, reviewed, deny-by-default policy instead of an undocumented void
// whose actual behavior nobody has tested. Update this file deliberately
// when that day comes; don't just add a client SDK and hope.
//
// NOT deployed by writing this file. Requires:
//   firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
// which is a live change to production security config and should be a
// deliberate decision, not something to run reflexively.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
